%S A400141 1,2,2,3,2,4,2,4,3,4,2,6,2,4,4,4,2,6,2,6,4,4,2,8,3,4,4,6,2,8,2,4,4,4,
%T A400141 4,9,2,4,4,8,2,8,2,6,6,4,2,8,3,6,4,6,2,8,4,8,4,4,2,12,2,4,6,7,4,8,2,6,
%U A400141 4,8,2,12,2,4,6,6,4,8,2,8,4,4,2,12,4,4,4,8,2,12
%N A400141 The number of divisors d of n such that gcid(d, n/d) is an exponentially odd number (A268335), where gcid is the greatest common infinitary divisor.
%C A400141 First differs from A365680, A366991, A399828, and A399831 at n = 32.
%C A400141 First differs from A252505 and A365173, A390555, and A400138 at n = 64.
%H A400141 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A400141/b400141.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A400141 Multiplicative with a(p^e) = A001316(e) if e is odd, and A001316(e) + e/2 if e is even.
%F A400141 Dirichlet g.f.: Product_{p prime} ((p^(2*s)/(p^(2*s)-1)^2) + Product_{k>=0} (1 + 2/p^(s*2^k))).
%t A400141 f[p_, e_] := 2^DigitSum[e, 2] + If[OddQ[e], 0, e/2];
%t A400141 a[1] = 1; a[n_] := Times @@ f @@@ FactorInteger[n]; Array[a, 100]
%o A400141 (PARI) f(e) = 2^hammingweight(e) + if(!(e % 2), e/2);
%o A400141 a(n) = vecprod(apply(f, factor(n)[, 2]));
%Y A400141 Cf. A001316, A268335, A365173 (analogous with gcd), A399831 (unitary analog), A400137.
%Y A400141 Cf. A252505, A390555.
%Y A400141 The number of divisors d of n such that gcid(d, n/d) is: A037445 (1), A400138 (squarefree), A400139 (powerful), A400140 (square), this sequence (exponentially odd), A400142 (odd), A400143 (power of 2).
%K A400141 nonn,mult,easy,new
%O A400141 1,2
%A A400141 _Amiram Eldar_, Sep 20 2026
